=== Introduction
--- The King and His Bride [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 45
sub: The King • The oil of gladness • Myrrh, aloes and cassia • The bride
refs: Psalm 45:2; Psalm 45:7; Psalm 45:11
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any reference to read the verse. Press C for the whole chapter.

=== The ready writer
--- A doctrine-rich wedding song
kicker: Psalm 45 · My heart is inditing a good matter
big: Psalm 45:1 | mark: my tongue is the pen of a ready writer
p: **Psalm 45** is a doctrine-rich wedding song, that speaks clearly of the Lord Jesus, a bride brought to Him, and the promise of exaltation for the King of Kings.
p: Of course, the dual nature is both a historic royal wedding song and a profound Messianic prophecy, the Psalms often capture both layers in a way that makes them relevant to our lives as believers. It is a testimony to their divine inspiration.

--- Inditing a good matter
kicker: Psalm 45 · Inspiration
p: The same inspiration described in the introduction found in verse 1.
p: The author says his heart is inditing a good matter, meaning fit to burst with the understanding he is speaking of. His *tongue is the pen of a ready writer.*
p: God has given him the idea, the words, His inspired, God breathed need to speak and record the word of God, that is bubbling out of his heart.

--- Moved by the Holy Ghost
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 45:1
p: Jesus said *out of the abundance of the heart the mouth speaketh*, be it the evil of the Pharisees or the inspired word. Whether the penman, or those who have committed the word to our hearts.
big: 2 Peter 1:21 | mark: holy men of God spake as they were moved by the Holy Ghost
p: And so, moved by the Holy Ghost, given the word of God, the ready writer gives us a song of romance and prophecy. Deeply layered and rich, as only scripture can be.
p: A similar song to Solomon's, messianic prophecy couched in a romantic allusion.

=== The King
--- Fairer than the children of men
kicker: Psalm 45 · The King
big: Psalm 45:2 | mark: Thou art fairer than the children of men
p: Verse 2 begins the description of One, fairer than all men, full of grace, blessed forever by God. The Christ. His chosen. The groom, and King.

--- Ride prosperously
kicker: Psalm 45 · O most mighty
scripture: Psalm 45:3-4 | mark: O most mighty; ride prosperously because of truth and meekness and righteousness
p: Girded with His sword, the most mighty. Almighty. A name of God given this person. All glory and majesty are His.
p: Riding majestically, and prosperously, upon a horse to battle.

--- Faithful and True
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 45:3–4
scripture: Revelation 19:11-13 | mark: behold a white horse; his name is called The Word of God

--- Thine arrows are sharp
kicker: Psalm 45 · The King's enemies
big: Psalm 45:5
p: He has the word of God proceeding from His lips. Destroying the enemies of God with His sharp sword. The strength of His right hand, teaching *thee terrible things*.

--- Truth and meekness and righteousness
kicker: Psalm 45 · Terrible things
p: What a picture that's painted. What a powerful, Almighty Son of God. Who comes to smite the enemies of His Father, and rule with a rod of iron, but His prosperity is in His truth and meekness and righteousness.
p: The terrible things, are in this context, the fearful lessons of the Lord. And these are grounded in those three qualities. The truth of the word, the meekness of our Savior, His righteousness in all things.

--- The sword of truth
kicker: Application • Truth
p: For the Christian, these three qualities are what we should pursue. We should gird ourselves with the sword of Truth, knowing His word in our hearts, ready to deliver it.
big: Hebrews 4:12 | mark: sharper than any twoedged sword

--- Meekness and righteousness
kicker: Application • Meekness and righteousness
p: Living in meekness, a servant. Condescending to men of low estate, that they may be saved. Putting our heart to the afflicted, the widow, the fatherless, the poor. For the meek shall inherit the Earth.
p: And living in righteousness. Doing right things by the judgment of God found in His word. Cleansed by the blood and carrying the righteousness of Christ within us. Being holy, as He is holy.

--- Thy throne, O God
kicker: Psalm 45 · The oil of gladness
scripture: Psalm 45:6-7 | mark: Thy throne, O God, is for ever and ever; Thou lovest righteousness, and hatest wickedness
p: Truth, meekness and righteousness means loving what is good and hating wickedness. This is the inheritance of the Lord and the perfection of it found in Christ Jesus.

--- But unto the Son he saith
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 45:6–7
scripture: Hebrews 1:8-9 | mark: But unto the Son he saith, Thy throne, O God, is for ever and ever
center: Christ, the Anointed One of God.

=== The anointing
--- Myrrh, and aloes, and cassia
kicker: Psalm 45 · Thy garments
big: Psalm 45:8 | mark: All thy garments smell of myrrh, and aloes, and cassia
p: After establishing His love for righteousness, the text states that He is anointed with the *oil of gladness* and that His garments smell of myrrh, aloes, and cassia.
p: As messianic prophecy, the elements of His anointment listed are symbols of Jesus, His death and His triumph over death.

--- The oil of gladness
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 45:7
p: The oil of gladness, olive oil, used to anoint kings and in celebration. To freshen the face, and to signify joy.
p: We see throughout scripture that oil is used as a metaphor for the Spirit. And Jesus, at the start of His ministry is anointed by the Spirit from the Father, showing to John and the other witnesses that Jesus was the Christ.
scripture: Matthew 3:16-17 | mark: the Spirit of God descending like a dove, and lighting upon him

--- Not by measure
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 45:7
p: While earthly kings were anointed with physical oil, Jesus was anointed directly by the Holy Spirit without measure.
scripture: John 3:33-35 | mark: God giveth not the Spirit by measure unto him

--- A name which is above every name
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 45:7
p: And so, the ultimate joy, the true anointing is given to Jesus the Christ.
scripture: Philippians 2:8-10 | mark: Wherefore God also hath highly exalted him
p: The meekness of the sinless Son of God, going to the cross, despising the shame to be raised into glory, as the King of righteousness. The truth by which we are set free.

--- The oil of joy for mourning
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 45:7
p: **Isaiah 61** says that He that was anointed by the Spirit of God to *preach good tidings to the meek..bind up the brokenhearted...proclaim liberty to the captives,* and to give unto them that trust in that truth..*.the oil of joy for mourning, the garment of praise for the spirit of heaviness; that they might be called trees of righteousness, the planting of the LORD, that he might be glorified.*
scripture: Isaiah 61:1-3 | mark: the oil of joy for mourning
p: The Messiah, the Anointed One, the Christ gives us the anointing of the Spirit by faith, that we may be His fruit, the trees of righteousness, planted that He might be glorified.

--- We have found the Messias
kicker: Cross references • The Christ
p: This is why, so frequently in the Gospels and Epistles, Christ is used as a kind of shorthand that carries all of this meaning in the context of Jesus being that Anointed One.
scripture: John 1:40-42 | mark: We have found the Messias, which is, being interpreted, the Christ
p: Andrew heard from John the Baptist, that Jesus was the Messias, the Christ, and immediately knew what that meant. He tells Simon Peter, who understands the implications.

--- Thou art the Christ
kicker: Cross references • The Christ
p: The term "Christ" was a heavily loaded, shorthand title that required no basic definition for a 1st-century, faithful, Jewish audience.
p: The concept of a coming, anointed deliverer was deeply woven into the fabric of the Jews' expectation, rooted in Old Testament prophecies of a King from the line of David, a Priest, or a Prophet, to deliver them.
p: In **Matthew 16:16**, Peter delivers his landmark declaration: *Thou art the Christ, the Son of the living God.*
big: Matthew 16:16

--- Art thou the Christ?
kicker: Cross references • The Christ
p: During Jesus' trial, the high priest asks him point-blank:
scripture: Mark 14:60-61 | mark: Art thou the Christ, the Son of the Blessed?
p: The religious leaders had an understanding of the scriptural definition of the Christ; they just believed Jesus was blasphemously claiming it.

--- That your joy may be full
kicker: Cross references • The Christ
p: But it is His to claim. And faith in this truth is what is required of man to be saved. The shorthand of the Christ, that carries so much spiritual weight.
big: 1 John 5:1 | mark: Whosoever believeth that Jesus is the Christ is born of God
p: And His anointing of joy is then given to we who believe, that our joy always be full.
scripture: 1 John 1:3-4 | mark: that your joy may be full

--- An holy anointing oil
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 45:8
p: The anointing oil is based on olive oil, but contains the other spices for fragrance, myrrh and cassia.
scripture: Exodus 30:22-25 | mark: pure myrrh; cassia; an holy anointing oil

--- Most holy
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 45:8
scripture: Exodus 30:26-29 | mark: that they may be most holy
p: This ointment is given of God to sanctify, and make all that it is applied to, holy. But it was reserved for the items of the Tabernacle and the High Priest, a holy anointing oil.

--- Upon man's flesh shall it not be poured
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 45:8
big: Exodus 30:32 | mark: Upon man’s flesh shall it not be poured
p: God also instructed not to anoint a stranger with this, as it is holy, threatening to cut off any who do it from the people.
p: Yet it is this holy oil, reserved for the things of God and His High Priest, that is given to the King described in **Psalm 45**.

--- Myrrh
kicker: Psalm 45 · Myrrh
p: Myrrh is a bitter resin extracted from a thorny tree, that was primarily used in burial preparations. It was given as a gift for a King by the wisemen visiting the infant Jesus.
p: Nicodemus used myrrh and aloes in the preparation of the body of Christ at His burial.
scripture: John 19:39-40 | mark: a mixture of myrrh and aloes

--- The scent of His ultimate act of love
kicker: Psalm 45 · Myrrh
p: There is a sense of the suffering of Christ, the death He tasted for all men, in the application of myrrh in the Psalm, reflected in the Gospels.
p: The scent of myrrh on the King carries with it His majesty, that cannot be separated from His sacrificial death. His garments carry the scent of His ultimate act of love.

--- Aloes
kicker: Psalm 45 · Aloes
p: The aloes mentioned are not the oil we know today, but the fragrance made from an eaglewood tree, and those aloes are found in Nicodemus' mixture. Because aloes were used to preserve bodies and counteract the smell of decay, they symbolize resurrection, healing, and incorruptibility of Christ, in His death.
big: Psalm 16:10 | mark: neither wilt thou suffer thine Holy One to see corruption

--- Cassia
kicker: Psalm 45 · Cassia
p: Cassia is an aromatic bark, like cinnamon, and is used in the holy anointing oil, mentioned above. This ties in to the understanding of Christ not only as King but High Priest.
p: Taken together, these three spices combined on the garments of the King paint a complete picture of the Gospel narrative. Myrrh as the sacrificial death, aloes His incorruption and resurrection, and cassia, Jesus' ascension, and role as High Priest, forever.
p: And so in **Psalm 45**, as the King approaches His bride, He carries with Him the literal aroma of redemption. The scent of His gospel. Amen

=== The bride
--- In gold of Ophir
kicker: Psalm 45 · The queen
big: Psalm 45:9 | mark: upon thy right hand did stand the queen in gold of Ophir
p: Continuing in **Psalm 45**, the messianic wedding song, we transition from the King to the bride.
p: At His right hand stands the queen, decked in gold from Ophir, (a site somewhere south-southwest of Jerusalem renowned for its gold).
p: Her ornamental beauty, provided by the King, to set apart the bride. The honorable women, the church.

--- Forget also thine own people
kicker: Psalm 45 · Hearken, O daughter
scripture: Psalm 45:10-11 | mark: forget also thine own people, and thy father’s house; So shall the king greatly desire thy beauty
p: As a picture of believers, we see the King's great desire for the beauty He has bestowed. He is truly Lord and we worship Him.
p: We are called to hear the appeal from Heaven, to consider and incline our ear (*faith cometh by hearing*), to forget our people and our parents, and to become His.

--- He cannot be my disciple
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 45:10
scripture: Luke 14:26-27 | mark: and hate not his father, and mother
p: We are called out, to be His above our family or national bonds. To "hate" here doesn't mean to dislike intensely, as it does today. It means to not love as much, to not prefer over another. To love Christ more than even your kin.

--- More than me
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 45:10
scripture: Matthew 10:35-37 | mark: He that loveth father or mother more than me is not worthy of me

--- Leave and cleave
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 45:10
p: Just as a wife is called to separate from her parents and cleave unto the husband.
big: Genesis 2:24 | mark: shall cleave unto his wife
p: Becoming the bride of Christ, cleaving unto Him, placing our love for Him above others. A wedding is a picture of this relationship that we have. It is given as a parable, that we may better know Him.

--- The rich among the people
kicker: Psalm 45 · The daughter of Tyre
big: Psalm 45:12 | mark: even the rich among the people shall intreat thy favour
p: In our status as His, the great of every nation shall show us favor. Perhaps in the Millennial Kingdom, certainly not in this life. But when we have come into the inheritance, they will respect our rule because Jesus is their Lord, and we are His.

--- All glorious within
kicker: Psalm 45 · The king's palace
scripture: Psalm 45:13-15 | mark: The king’s daughter is all glorious within; With gladness and rejoicing shall they be brought
p: We are arrayed as a bride in gold and fine embroidery, the white garments of purity, the righteousness of Christ that covers us.
big: Luke 15:10 | mark: there is joy in the presence of the angels of God over one sinner that repenteth
p: This is the celebration of salvation. And the future wedding supper we are called to.

--- Princes in all the earth
kicker: Psalm 45 · Thy children
big: Psalm 45:16 | mark: Instead of thy fathers shall be thy children
p: We will not look to our earthly heritage, the genealogies and lineage, but instead at those we have won to the Gospel, as children who rule and reign with Christ. It is our godly heritage, the spiritual children we present to Christ.
p: Looking forward to the future of those we have helped come to repentance. This is our vow to our Lord and Savior.

--- In all generations
kicker: Psalm 45 · For ever and ever
big: Psalm 45:17
center: Amen

